WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.679 --> 00:00:13.929
When I look at just one picture from Simon
Stålenhag’s Tales from the Loop, I feel

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:16.900
like I’ve watched an entire film.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.070
In a single, haunting image, a novel’s worth
of narrative seems to play out just below

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.710
the surface.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:28.940
If you’ve ever seen this art before, you’ve
probably experienced this too — even if

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:31.759
you didn’t know where it was from.

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:36.750
Tales from the Loop and its sequel Things
from the flood are worldbuilding projects

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:43.040
set in an alternate version of Sweden in the
80s and 90s, in which a gigantic particle

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:49.950
accelerator buried beneath the fields and
suburbs begins to alter reality in unpredictable

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.070
ways.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:54.540
Yet the story is so much more than a summary
can convey.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:00.770
There’s a reason why Stålenhag’s work
has been adapted into an RPG, a board game,

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.379
and even a live action tv show.

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:09.700
It is a haunting, atmospheric project that
swallows you whole — and the more scenes

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:15.080
you observe, the more you can piece together
the engrossing narrative below the surface.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:21.310
So, for this entry into the archive, I want
to dive deep into the hidden lore of Tales

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.220
from the Loop — and investigate why this
project has struck a chord with myself and

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.900
so many others.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:33.030
This video will explore the art Stålenhag
has released online, but you can purchase

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.880
the full artbook using the links in the description.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:43.030
Now, let’s enter the world of Simon Stålenhag,
and discover the mysteries of Tales from the

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:47.909
Loop.

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:53.170
The distorted timeline of Tales from the Loop
begins with the Loop itself — the largest

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:59.580
particle accelerator in the world, built to
be so powerful it can warp space-time.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.990
Constructed in the 1960s, the Loop’s presence
can be felt everywhere in the surrounding

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:11.000
Swedish countryside — not just in daily
vibrations and mysterious anomalies, but also

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.760
in literal changes to the landscape.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.980
While the Loop itself is underground, monolithic
structures and heaps of scrap metal connected

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.819
to the facility gradually overtake the region.

00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:29.139
By the 1980s — the period in which Tale
of the Loop is set — the colossal cooling

00:02:29.139 --> 00:02:35.280
towers of the central reactor are almost always
in view, with their green lights casting an

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.609
eerie glow into the foggy skies.

00:02:38.609 --> 00:02:44.730
These scenes, while beautiful, also evoke
a hard-to-place feeling of anxiety, as the

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:51.290
looming structures increasingly overshadow
the seemingly oblivious townsfolk.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:57.870
Soon, various advancements begin to seep their
way into everyday life.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:03.540
Magnetrine vehicles — crafts that can float
thanks to a newly discovered levitating force

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.299
— become increasingly commonplace in the
world of the Loop.

00:03:08.299 --> 00:03:14.069
In the coming decades, several thousand-ton
freighters take to the skies, appearing in

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:20.739
the far background of various scenes, their
artificial lights like distant stars.

00:03:20.739 --> 00:03:25.769
The scale echoes the megalithic 80s spaceships
from films like ‘Close Encounters of the

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:29.909
Third Kind,’ with their vast landscapes
of flickering light.

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:36.079
Yet there’s a sense of quiet, unmistakable
dread mixed in with the nostalgia, as these

00:03:36.079 --> 00:03:40.249
ships cast a shadow over scenes of ordinary
lives.

00:03:40.249 --> 00:03:45.569
Stålenhag seems to be telling us that reality
around the facility is changing, with the

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:52.089
facility’s imperceptible influence leaking
into all aspects of society.

00:03:52.089 --> 00:03:58.120
And Magnetrine vehicles aren’t the most
revolutionary machines.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.379
Breakthroughs in artificial nervous systems
allow for machines that walk with a balance

00:04:03.379 --> 00:04:07.169
typically only seen in the natural organisms.

00:04:07.169 --> 00:04:13.439
Factories produce multilegged machines for
all purposes — from forestry to mining to

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:14.760
warfare.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:20.130
The region surrounding the Loop becomes a
hub for unmanned units, with strange machines

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:22.940
roaming the woods and backcountry.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:29.120
Like the towers and hover-ships, these robots
create a distinct feeling of… wrongness,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.789
for lack a better word.

00:04:30.789 --> 00:04:38.940
The land itself feels alien and unwelcoming,
a place where the ordinary doesn’t belong.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:44.770
While Tales from the Loop takes primary inspiration
from 80s media, aesthetically the project

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:49.860
also echoes the enigmatic 70s sci-fi film
Stalker.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:55.639
Directed by Andrei Tarkovsky, Stalker tells
the story of three men who venture across

00:04:55.639 --> 00:04:59.500
a mysterious restricted site known simply
as the "Zone", an area in which the normal

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:00.569
laws of physics do not apply.

00:05:00.569 --> 00:05:05.740
At the epicenter of the Zone there exists
a room which can supposedly grant a person's

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:06.740
innermost desires.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:07.740
It’s… a whole thing, to put it bluntly.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.650
The film is dense, complex, and endlessly
interpretable — but one could argue that

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:16.810
above all, Stalker is a film about atmosphere.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:21.539
Much like Tales from the Loop, the natural
green landscapes of the Zone aren’t what

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:24.940
we typically associate with sci fi scenery.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:30.509
Yet through wide shots and the subtle presence
of industrial debris, the countryside of the

00:05:30.509 --> 00:05:36.389
Zone feels menacing and distorted — but
in a way that is difficult to rationalize.

00:05:36.389 --> 00:05:41.889
While conceptually Tales from the Loop and
Stalker diverge in various ways, this concept

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:47.810
of familiar landscapes rendered unfamiliar
through quiet abnormalities is something central

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:52.229
to both works.

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:57.990
But all the technology we’ve seen so far
in Tales from the Loop is just the beginning.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:03.319
As the decade wears on, whispers begin to
circulate that the facility is attempting

00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:07.210
to create machines able to feel and reason.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:13.650
Some of these prototypes even manage to escape,
while others are disposed of unceremoniously.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:21.300
Androids are machines designed to look particularly
human, and many find themselves rusting in

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:27.520
forgotten corners of the land, just one of
many strange objects the Loop’s operations

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.900
leave behind.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.590
And there are rumors of stranger occurrences.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.930
Prehistoric monsters begin to appear in the
woods.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:45.819
In the wake of cosmic energies, a bridge is
created, allowing creatures from the distant

00:06:45.819 --> 00:06:48.520
past to wander into the present.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.789
In one image, a group of theropods wander
across the countryside near an empty road,

00:06:53.789 --> 00:06:57.150
while arching structures loom in the background.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:02.669
The feeling of out-of-placeness that these
paintings evoke is heightened, as their subjects

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:05.240
are out-of-time as well.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.610
These dinosaurs have wonderfully retro design
sensibilities, mirroring what you might see

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:16.289
in the original Jurassic Park films or on
old-school dinosaur posters.

00:07:16.289 --> 00:07:21.430
In another scene, a group of Hadrosaurids
roam through the snow near the rusting shells

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:27.050
of abandoned Magnetrine vehicles, confused
by this strange new land.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:31.750
While an exciting image, there’s also a
sense in pieces like these that the Loop is

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:37.199
changing things forever — that these upsets
to the natural order might be too great to

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:39.500
be made right again.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:47.250
Notably, children feature prominently in most
Tales from the Loop images, and indeed the

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:52.610
entire volume is positioned as the childhood
memories of the author’s alter ego.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:58.639
A childhood near the facility means these
children are exposed to many things beyond

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:01.870
their comprehension, which they treat as miraculous.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:06.860
They don’t understand how irrevocably the
world around them is changing.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:08.169
How could they?

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:14.340
I think on some level, Tales from the Loop
is a story about the cultural memory of the

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:21.039
80s, in which the sci-fi imagery from that
decade assumes literal, physical form.

00:08:21.039 --> 00:08:28.480
In this regard, it shares a lot of DNA with
another 80s sci-fi inspired film, Panos Cosmatos’s

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.560
Beyond the Black Rainbow.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:37.900
Released in 2010 but set in 1983, the film
focuses on a secluded research facility called

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:44.200
the Arboria Institute, which houses all sorts
of surreal and unethical experiments.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:50.029
Over the course of the film, the protagonist
— a girl with latent psychic abilities imprisoned

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:55.320
within the lower levels — slowly becomes
more and more aware of the horrors that the

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:57.769
facility really contains.

00:08:57.769 --> 00:09:03.910
The psychedelic narrative moves at a deliberately
slow, hypnotic pace — with much of the plot

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.950
coming purely from visual storytelling.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:12.660
The retro imagery feels born from the same
primordial soup as Tales from the Loop, with

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:16.019
most scenes awash with diffused neon light.

00:09:16.019 --> 00:09:21.250
Yet while Beyond the Black Rainbow is ultimately
a story about the loss of innocence and the

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:26.330
destruction of childhood, in Tales from the
Loop, the children never ‘wake up’ to

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.440
the terrors of the facility.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:33.339
Through the lens of childhood, the miracles
of the Loop are just that — miracles.

00:09:33.339 --> 00:09:39.750
It’s only us, the audience, that are aware
of how dysfunctional things really are.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:48.149
As a result, as unnerving as the artbook can
get, it never veers into a full-on nightmare.

00:09:48.149 --> 00:09:56.459
Probably because the real nightmares show
up in Things from the Flood.

00:09:56.459 --> 00:10:00.820
Released two years after Tales from the Loop,
Things from the Flood is a follow-up artbook

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:06.920
that takes place in the 1990s after the Loop
is supposedly decommissioned — but the strange

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:09.010
occurrences continue.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:14.779
In Things from the Flood, children are now
teenagers, anguishing from the disillusionment

00:10:14.779 --> 00:10:16.839
that comes with growing up.

00:10:16.839 --> 00:10:22.740
If the first volume is like a dream, the second
is like waking up, and finding the world is

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.930
much less simple than you realized.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:29.889
Things from the Flood begins with disaster.

00:10:29.889 --> 00:10:34.810
The titular flood comes without warning — a
leak from the innards of the decommissioned

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:41.870
loop that floods basements, buries cars, and
leaves towns surrounding the facility half-drowned.

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:47.259
The immediate effect following the flood is
displacement, with the government moving families

00:10:47.259 --> 00:10:52.560
whose homes were destroyed by the liquid into
more urban areas further away from the decommissioned

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:53.560
loop.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:11:00.459
Here, Stålenhag brings to life a landscape
of exceeding bleak and impersonal office buildings,

00:11:00.459 --> 00:11:03.710
which have a disquieting, liminal quality.

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:09.690
Some of these urban landscapes feature colossal
vertical cities: surreal towers consisting

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:17.460
of numerous conjoined buildings — the product
of a public housing program from decades earlier.

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:23.250
One noteworthy element that lurks at the edges
of these sequences are nameless mascots — whose

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:29.610
overly-cheerful and commercialized nature
contrasts bitterly with the gloomy and dower

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:30.610
landscapes.

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:36.230
Their exaggerated smiles reflect the prevailing
ethos around the Loop that ‘everything is

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:41.970
fine,’ although expressions seem strained
and unsettling in such a grim world.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:47.980
Decaying mascots that continue to smile despite
the ruin creeping in around them are a motif

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:53.740
present in Stålenhag’s art in general,
and I think strike a chord with a lot of people.

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:59.149
There’s something fundamentally mesmerizing
about these images, which give the impression

00:11:59.149 --> 00:12:01.340
of a quiet dystopia.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:06.779
And it’s not just mascots that help create
this atmosphere.

00:12:06.779 --> 00:12:11.540
Painted flowers adorn the sides of bare concrete
blocks — and apparent attempt to make up

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:14.540
for what the material world is lacking.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:20.190
At night, the city itself seems threatening,
lit by neon advertisements suspended above

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:23.610
the skyline by Magnetrine technology.

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:28.380
Scenes like this show hyper-commercialism
creeping into the world of Things from the

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:34.610
Flood, exerting an influence as subtle and
insidious as the loop itself.

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:40.640
Discarded robots also feature heavily in these
illustrations, rusting away in the back alleys

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:43.810
of the city.

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:48.170
On the margins of urban areas, a new population
emerges.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:53.820
Androids fleeing from demolition sites settle
in the outskirts of the forests.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:59.509
These displaced machines are called Vagabonds,
and more advanced that the prototypes of the

00:12:59.509 --> 00:13:04.670
previous decade — showing an interest in
colorful clothing and complex patterns.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:11.079
There’s an unmistakable despair and desperation
to many of these portraits of Vagabonds — a

00:13:11.079 --> 00:13:16.350
feeling that these are intelligent beings
pushed to the outskirts of a society that

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.330
sees them as nuts and bolts.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:23.920
Clearly, these are machines capable of the
self-expression and individualism we typically

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:29.660
associate only with ourselves, yet according
to passages from Things from the Flood, most

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:35.020
people think of these machines as a nuisance
— or outright fear them.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:39.760
In truth, their fears should be directed elsewhere.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:45.680
A sickness overtakes some of the larger machines
— a kind of infection that causes them to

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.459
roam around like queasy animals.

00:13:48.459 --> 00:13:53.370
Bizarre, organic-look growths begin to form
on their limbs and joints.

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:58.490
Soon, the government is working around the
clock trying to contain these nightmarish

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:00.160
rouge machines.

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:05.170
The official explanation is that the neural
grease that allows their artificial nervous

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:11.089
systems to function has mutated, resulting
in a grim-looking but ultimately harmless

00:14:11.089 --> 00:14:12.089
growths.

00:14:12.089 --> 00:14:17.070
But if one reads between the margins, another
story emerges: that something from the flood

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:24.360
has leaked into the groundwater, which reacts
to inorganic material in disturbing ways.

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:27.829
For it’s not just large machines that the
sickness is affecting.

00:14:27.829 --> 00:14:34.110
In a striking series of images, we can see
how smaller objects made of plastic and metal

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:37.310
mutate into semi-organic forms.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:42.760
In some pieces it seems like something is
growing inside the empty husks and spilling

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:48.230
out through the cracks, whereas in others
it seems like the objects themselves are melting

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.410
away into a living mass.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:56.199
There’s a real terror to some of these images
— but also something morbidly engrossing,

00:14:56.199 --> 00:15:02.290
like the pictures themselves are drawing you
in closer, asking you to ponder every disquieting

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:03.420
detail.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:08.720
The objects chosen are instantly familiar
from our commercial reality, and yet they

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:14.380
take on an entirely new meaning when presented
in this context.

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:20.410
It’s fitting that Things from the Flood,
a story about growing up from the childhood

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:26.740
shown in Tales from the Loop, seem to take
inspiration from much darker forms of media.

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:32.389
Aesthetically, this section seems to pay homage
to the bodily terror of another 80s sci-fi

00:15:32.389 --> 00:15:36.180
film: David Cronenberg’s Videodrome.

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:41.879
Not a work for the faint of heart, the disturbing
cult classic follows the CEO of a television

00:15:41.879 --> 00:15:47.649
station who stumbles upon a broadcast signal
showing various criminal acts, and begins

00:15:47.649 --> 00:15:53.300
to lose touch with reality as the world around
him seems to become disfigured.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:59.420
As is the case in Stålenhag’s work, everyday
objects begin to writhe like living organisms,

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:05.209
with the inorganic and sanitized taking on
the properties of the organic and visceral.

00:16:05.209 --> 00:16:11.170
To parse the exact meaning behind Videodrome’s
madness would be… an impossible undertaking

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:17.290
in this time frame, but broadly the film seems
to explore concepts of how violence intertwines

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:23.870
with entertainment and how humans can become
desensitized to visceral horrors.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:29.819
You can see similar themes of desensitization
at play in Things from the Flood, as the public

00:16:29.819 --> 00:16:35.860
fails to question the source of the mutations
or even be appropriately horrified by them.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:37.300
They’ve seen it all before.

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:44.560
Life near the Loop is full of quiet, everyday
terrors.

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:49.870
But in other images, certain infected machines
seem to be evolving.

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:55.190
In some of Stålenhag’s art from Things
from the Flood, we can see almost entirely

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:58.770
organic beings menacing the countryside.

00:16:58.770 --> 00:17:04.400
Even more so than usual, there’s an ambiguity
to many of these sequences.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:10.860
One thing seems clear, however: these fleshy
tendrils and strange growths are growing bolder…

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:11.860
and more autonomous.

00:17:11.860 --> 00:17:17.260
In some compositions, they seem more like
independent lifeforms than parasites tied

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.580
to machines.

00:17:19.580 --> 00:17:24.640
One of my absolute favorite pieces of art
from the Things from the Flood series shows

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:28.710
a small car menaced by two massive beings.

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:34.540
The piece seems to be related to the machine
illness, with the creatures posing similarly

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:41.430
fleshy tentacles, but Stålenhag doesn’t
reveal the exact story behind these lifeforms,

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:44.790
which is part of what makes the image so engrossing.

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:49.750
As is often the case with Things from the
Flood, the ambiguity makes the worldbuilding

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:53.770
all the more intriguing.

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:59.920
Just how Tales from the Loop represents childhood
with its nostalgic atmosphere and more innocent

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:05.910
viewpoint, Things from the Flood is a distinctly
teenage work — all the way down to uncomfortable

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:07.460
red pimples.

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:13.290
Yet at the same time, the story is more than
just melancholy, showing the bright moments

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:16.240
in growing up as well as the dark ones.

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:21.950
I certainly won’t give away the ending to
Things from the Flood in this video, but suffice

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:27.410
to say the narrative continues to embody the
themes Stålenhag established throughout his

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:29.160
larger body of work.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:33.711
If you’d like to know more of the specifics
of what that entails — you’ll have to

00:18:33.711 --> 00:18:39.590
check out the books for yourself…

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:43.730
Tales from the Loop and Things from the Flood
are both haunting and hopeful.

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:50.140
They tell small, ordinary stories in the margins
of massive, world-shaking events.

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:56.200
With solitary images and minimal text, Simon
Stålenhag conjures a world that feels like

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:59.790
looking into the future and the past at the
